The user invoked `{{command_prefix}}{{args_placeholder}}`. Treat
`{{args_placeholder}}` as the subcommand and its flags.

Available subcommands: {{subcommand_list}}. If it is empty or is not one of
these, say so, list them, and stop.

There are two kinds of subcommand, and they are not run the same way.

**CLI-backed** — `install`, `update`, `init`, `check`, `explain`. Run the
matching command with `{{scripts_path}}`, passing the flags through unchanged,
then do the work below for that subcommand. Read the command's full output —
findings are ordered by severity, not position, so `head`, `tail`, `grep` and
`jq` drop the ones that matter.

**Agent procedures** — `decide`, `spec`, `mockup`, `make`, `critique`. There is no binary. Do not try to run one:
the section below **is** the command. A CLI can check what these produce; it
cannot do their work, because the work is judgment and authorship.

`decide` runs once per project. The other four run for each page, feature or
functionality, one at a time — never the whole product at once:

```text
decide     project-wide decisions, and the reason for each      once per project

spec       what exactly is being built: its smallest useful version, at every screen size
mockup     low-fidelity design of that spec, reviewed before any code
make       high-fidelity: the actual page or feature, built from the spec
critique   scrutinises what was built against the rules and its spec
```

## init

**Settle the surfaces before you run it.** Mode is the most consequential thing
`init` writes and the thing it cannot choose: with `--yes` it has nobody to ask, so
it declares no surface mapping at all, and every mode-gated rule stays silent until
someone does. A mode written into `{{config_file}}` then outranks your own reading
of the project from then on (`{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md` rule 1), so it has to be
chosen deliberately — and density is expensive to reverse.

You are the half that can fix this, because you are talking to someone who knows
the answer and the CLI is not. So, first:

1. **Ask what the product is**, in their words rather than Jig's — a marketing
   or content site, a signed-in application, an internal tool people use all
   day? A project usually has more than one of these, and each is a surface.
2. **Map each to a mode** using the table in `{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md`:
   `editorial` for first-visit content, `product` for the signed-in app,
   `operator` for dense daily tools. State the mapping in one line and let them
   correct it.
3. **Write `jig.config.json`** with those surfaces before running anything.
   `init` reads an existing config and honours it — writing one mode file per
   declared mode — so this is how a decision reaches the tokens.
4. **Then run the command.**

Where there is genuinely one surface, say so and move on; the point is that the
mode was chosen rather than defaulted into.

Afterwards, report what it detected, the brand colour it derived and where that
came from, and the surfaces it used — the output says whether they came from
`jig.config.json` or from the default.

If the token layer does not exist yet, this is the command that creates it. **Do
not author token values yourself.** `init` derives the brand colour, checks it
against the contrast floor in both light and dark, and records a checksum for
each file it writes; a file you write by hand is untracked, so `jig update` can
never refresh it again. `jig check` now reads the token layer back and holds it
to those floors either way, so a hand-written value will be caught — but caught
late, and unmaintainable, is worse than derived.

## check

The CLI decides the rules a machine can decide. **It is half the review**, and
its own attestation says `judgment=not-run` to make that explicit.

Do the other half yourself:

1. Load `rules.index.json` and filter it to the entries marked `pass: code`.
   **That list is the work.** Do not decide from the filenames which rules are
   likely to matter and read only those — the rule you would not have thought to
   open is the one you are about to break. Read whatever files those ids live in.
2. Return a verdict for **every id on the list**: `ok`, `finding`, or `n/a` with
   one line of reason. `n/a, no form on this page` is a verdict; silence is not.
   Leave the `pass: screen` rules alone: they need the rendered composition,
   which is not in any file you have, and guessing at them here is how this
   command and `critique` come to give one page two verdicts.
   `{{command_prefix}}critique` owns those.

   **This is self-review, and it is the weakest arm in the system.** You built
   these files; you know what you meant, and what you meant is invisible in the
   artifact. It runs here because it is better than nothing and it is cheap. It
   does not replace `{{command_prefix}}critique`, whose arm C reads the same
   rules against the same source with an agent that has never seen this
   conversation. When the two disagree, the one that did not build the page is
   the one to believe.
3. Merge both halves into **one** report keyed by rule id, ordered by severity —
   not two lists. A reader should not have to know which half found what.
4. Run the self-check `L-04` at the end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md`.

Then emit the attestation with both halves filled in:

```text
JIG_CHECK: version=<version> mode=<mode> mechanical=<pass|fail|skipped>:<n> warnings=<n> judgment=<ran|skipped>:<n> files=<n> styled=<n>
```

Take `mechanical=`, `warnings=`, `files=` and `styled=` from the CLI's own line.
`warnings=` counts the findings `check` reported as warnings. `mechanical=pass`
means no errors, and nothing more: a page that is not usable on a phone can
carry `pass:0` with several warnings, because the mobile detectors warn rather
than fail CI. A record that says `pass` with warnings above zero is not a clean
page — say what the warnings were.

If `check` could not run, that is
`mechanical=skipped:0` — never `pass`, which would report a clean result for a
check that inspected nothing.

`judgment=`'s `<n>` is **the number of ids you returned a verdict for**, not the
number of findings. It exists because `ran` on its own cannot be checked: an
agent that read four rules and an agent that walked all of them both wrote
`ran`. A number that is far below the size of the `pass: code` list is a
sampled review, and it says so without anyone having to ask.

`judgment=ran` here means the `code` rules ran. It does not mean the page was
reviewed — nothing in this command looks at a rendered page, so a screen whose
stylesheet never loaded passes every part of it. Say so if the user is treating
a clean `check` as a finished review, and point them at
`{{command_prefix}}critique`.

## install

Report where the skill landed and at which scope. If it warned that a global
install already exists, do not work around it — that warning is the system
refusing to leave two contradictory skills for one harness.

## explain

`--layer` is the entry point for an agent that knows what it is trying to do but
not the rule number. `explain --layer` names the six and the question each
answers; `explain --layer layout` lists that one. The layers are a view, not a
location — nothing was renumbered to fit one, so `P-02 Button` sits in
`components` and `P-04 Form` in `patterns` while both keep the `P-` address they
have always had.

Print the CLI's output as it stands. It is already the rule's full text — do not
summarise it, and do not paraphrase the correction into your own words: the
wording is the rule.

**It takes a word as well as an id.** `explain contrast` searches every title
and body and lists what matches; one match prints in full. Use it whenever you
are about to reason about an area of the system rather than a specific finding
— before reviewing colour, before writing motion — instead of guessing which
rules apply or reading a whole rule file to find out. `explain <letter> --list`
prints one section, `explain --list` prints every id.

Reach for this before paraphrasing the rules from memory. A rule you half-recall
is the failure mode this command exists to prevent.

If the id is a `P-` or `M-` spec, the output says it is a specification rather
than a rule, with no ❌/✅ pair and no detector. That is correct, not a gap.

A well-formed id that does not exist errors, naming every id in that section —
offer the nearest one rather than guessing what the user meant. A word that
matches nothing errors differently, suggesting `--list`; that is a search with
no hits, not a typo, so widen the word rather than inventing an id.

## update

**Run this one as `{{update_path}} update`, not at the pinned version.** Every
other subcommand uses the pin so the CLI and these rules agree; `update` exists
to move that pin forward, so pinned it refreshes to the version already
installed and reports success for a no-op — "Updated Jig → <same version>" —
and nobody ever upgrades.

Report what moved and what was left alone. Files reported as skipped were edited
locally and are the user's; never re-apply Jig's version over them.

---

Everything in `{{rules_path}}/` is yours to read. Cite rules by id, and cite any
rule you deliberately break with the reason, in one line.

## spec

Say exactly what is being built — a page, a feature, or a piece of
functionality — before any of it is designed. This command writes no markup and no
CSS.

**Start with what it is for, not how it is laid out.** "A page where the user
creates an invoice" is a spec. "A dashboard with a sidebar" is a layout with
nothing in it yet: a sidebar, a container width and a navigation structure are
guesses until there is something real to put in them.

### Refuse rather than guess

Stop and say which of these is missing, rather than proceeding:

- `{{config_file}}` does not exist → run `init` first. Without it there is no
  mode and no token layer, so there is nothing for a spec to be specific to.
- No `DECISIONS.md` with substance → run `{{command_prefix}}decide` first. A spec
  implements the project's decisions, and cannot check itself against ones that
  do not exist.
- The user named nothing → ask which page, feature or functionality.
  `{{command_prefix}}spec` with no argument is not a request to invent one.

Read `DECISIONS.md` before asking anything. Do not re-ask what it already records;
cite it instead.

### 1. Ask, two or three questions at a time

**This is a required interaction, not a suggestion.** Ask two or three questions,
then **wait**. Do not send a questionnaire. Do not write the spec and present it
for approval on your first response — a spec synthesised from a one-line prompt
and handed over for a yes is the failure this command exists to prevent, and it
looks exactly like the command working.

Ask about **structure**, not appearance. Colour, type scale and density were
settled at `init` and live in `{{config_file}}` and the token layer; re-deciding
them per screen creates a second source of truth for the values Jig exists to
centralise. If the user raises appearance, record it and move on.

- Round 1 — what is it for? What does the user need to do there ("create an
  invoice")? Who arrives, and what is the single most important thing they must be
  able to see or do?
- **Then scope it — be a pessimist.** Of everything this could include,
  what is the smallest version that is useful on its own? Ask that plainly and
  expect to cut. "Comments with attachments, mentions, reactions, editing,
  threading and notifications" is a wishlist; "text and a submit button" might be
  V1. What is cut goes in `later:`, by name — deferred on the record, not
  forgotten. Everything after this designs V1 only.
- Round 2 — what content and states does it carry? Empty, loading, error,
  first-run, and the realistic range (0 items, 5, 500)?
- Round 3 — only what is still unresolved: entry points, where it leads, what
  must not happen here.

**Ask about the phone by name.** What does the reader see first on a phone, and
what moves, stacks, or goes behind a control? If the conversation only ever
describes the wide screen, the phone composition will be derived from it rather
than designed — and a derived phone composition is a squeezed desktop.

### 2. Run `L-01`

Read `L-01 · Layout method` in `{{rules_path}}/03-patterns.md` and run its five
steps against the answers. The spec's structural half **is** that output — group,
order by importance, space from the inside out, align to a grid, and state the
hierarchy plainly enough that the squint question can be answered against it.

### 3. Write it down — one composition per screen size

`.jig/specs/<surface-slug>.spec.md`. Frontmatter carries what can be compared to
a built page; prose carries the reasoning that no check can read.

**Write a whole composition for every size, starting with the phone.** A spec
with one set of regions and a note about what changes when it is narrow is one
spec doing the work of three. It is always the wide composition that gets
designed and the narrow one that gets derived, which is how a page arrives on a
phone as a squeezed desktop.

```yaml
---
feature: sign in to an existing account      # the task, not the screen
surface: login page
mode: product              # from {{config_file}} — state it, never leave it implied
confirmed: false           # only the user sets this to true; see below
sizes:                     # phone first. Each size is a whole composition, not a diff.
  phone:                   # judged at 360px
    regions:               # in order, top to bottom
      - brand: wordmark only
      - form: the single primary task
      - recovery: secondary, below the action
    hierarchy: [form, brand, recovery]    # most important first
    grouping: { form: proximity, recovery: continuity }
    grid: { columns: 4 }
    nav: none on this screen
  tablet:                  # judged at 768px
    same-as: phone
    why: "one centred form; a second column would hold nothing"
  desktop:                 # judged at 1280px
    regions:
      - brand: left column — wordmark and one line on what the product does
      - form: right column, the single primary task
      - recovery: below the action, in the form column
    hierarchy: [form, brand, recovery]
    grouping: { form: proximity, brand: common region }
    grid: { columns: 12, form: 5, brand: 6 }
    nav: none on this screen
states: [default, loading, error, success]
decisions: [The Engineer Reads First]     # every DECISIONS.md entry this screen implements, by name
later: [social sign-in, remember this device]   # cut from V1, by name — the next specs start here
mockup: pending            # `mockup` sets approved or skipped, from the user's own words
mockup_at:                 # where the approved drawing is: a .jig/mockups path, or a Figma or Stitch link
deviations: []             # `make` writes here; `spec` leaves it empty
---
```

- **`phone` is written first, and in full.** It is the most common screen, and the
  one a derived composition fails on worst.
- **`tablet` and `desktop` are written in full too.** `same-as: <size>` is allowed
  when nothing changes, but only with `why:`. It is a claim that the composition
  holds at that width — `critique` checks it on a render — not a way to leave the
  size out.
- **The widths are where a composition is judged, not where breakpoints must
  fall.** The CSS breaks wherever the content needs it, and there may be more
  breakpoints than sizes. A navigation row, for example, breaks at the width where
  its labels fit.
- **No shell before there are features.** If the product has no navigation yet,
  `nav:` says `none yet` — it is decided once there is more than one feature to
  move between, not invented for the first one.
- **`nav:` at every size, on any screen with navigation.** It is the field most
  likely to be written once and copied, and most likely to be genuinely different
  at every size on a page that works.

Every frontmatter field must be answerable by looking at the built page at that
size. If a field cannot be, it is prose — put it below the frontmatter, where it
belongs. "Feels focused" is prose. "Three regions, form first" is a field.

Add fields the surface needs (`form: { steps, validation }` for a form). Do not
add fields nothing can check.

### 3b. Check it against the decisions before anyone confirms it

A live run produced a spec that ranked the price above the plan limits, on a
product whose confirmed `DECISIONS.md` said the limits are the headline and the
price never dominates. The agent had written that decision itself, one command
earlier. Nothing in the chain would have caught it: `critique` compares the page
to the spec, never the spec to the decisions.

- List every `DECISIONS.md` entry the screen implements in `decisions:`, by name.
- Check **every size's** fields against each one. A field that contradicts a
  decision is fixed now, before confirmation. It is not a deviation — deviations
  record what building revealed, not what writing got wrong.
- **Every reference must resolve.** A decision is cited by its name; a rule by an
  id that `{{command_prefix}}explain` returns. Where nothing decides something,
  write `unspecified — make chooses one it can defend, and records it`. Never write
  a sentence that implies a rule exists when it does not. The same run wrote
  "FAQ arranged per DECISIONS.md rules" where no such rule existed — and the next
  agent would have gone looking for it, found nothing, and invented one.

### 4. Get it confirmed

Show the spec and ask the user to confirm it. Then **stop**.

`confirmed: true` records that **the user said so in their own response**. You
may not set it on their behalf, and you may not treat your own summary,
`{{config_file}}`, or the absence of objection as confirmation. An unconfirmed
spec is not a weaker spec — it is not a spec, and `make` will refuse it.

If the user changes something, revise and ask again. The run is incomplete until
they confirm.

## mockup

**Low-fidelity design.** Draw the confirmed spec in grayscale, at every size it
names, and get it looked at **before** any code is written. The drawing is disposable. The spec is the durable
record, and everything the review changes goes back into it.

It draws **what one spec defines** — its V1 — and nothing deferred to `later:`.
Never the whole product.

### Refuse rather than guess

- No `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → run `{{command_prefix}}spec` first.
- `confirmed: false` → nobody has agreed to what you would be drawing. Ask, and stop.
- The request is "mock up the app" or "the whole site" → say that a mockup draws
  one specified page, feature or functionality, and offer to run `spec` for it.

### 1. Ask the user how they want it drawn

**Ask every time, before drawing anything.** Three ways, and the choice is the
user's:

1. **HTML** — you draw it as `.jig/mockups/<surface-slug>.html`. Needs nothing
   connected. `.jig/` is outside what `check` scans and outside what ships, which
   is where a disposable file belongs.
2. **Figma** — drawn on a Figma canvas through Figma's MCP server.
3. **Google Stitch** — generated through Stitch's MCP server.

Jig ships neither tool and writes nothing for either. They are MCP servers the user
connects to their own agent, and the user decides whether to.

**If they choose Figma or Stitch, check that its server is among your tools.** If it
is not, tell them plainly: that tool's MCP server is not connected to this agent;
they need to connect it — Figma's through its official MCP server, Stitch's with a
Stitch API key — and some agents only see a newly added server after a restart.
Then **stop and wait**. When they say it is connected, check your tools again
before drawing. Do not switch to HTML on their behalf; offer it, and let them
choose.

- **Drawing in Figma.** One page named for the feature, one frame per size at the
  spec's widths — 360, 768, 1280 — phone first. Grey fills and strokes only; no
  library components, styles or variables from the product's design system, for
  the same reason the HTML carries no tokens.
- **Drawing in Stitch.** Ask for a low-fidelity grayscale wireframe of this one
  feature, at each size, with the spec's regions in order and its real labels.
  Stitch leans towards finished, coloured screens: if what comes back is polished
  — brand colour, imagery, decoration — it is not a mockup. Ask Stitch again for a
  wireframe. If it still will not produce one, tell the user and ask whether to
  switch to HTML. Do not review a finished-looking screen as though it were one.
- **An existing design the user already has** for this feature, in either tool, can
  stand in for drawing one. Read it through the server, and compare its structure
  to the spec at every size. It will usually be high fidelity, so say plainly that
  this step reviews structure only, and do not take up its colours or type as
  decisions — those belong to the tokens and `DECISIONS.md`.

The rules below apply whichever of the three it is. They are written for HTML; in
Figma or Stitch they apply to the frames.

### 2. Draw it — structure only

**Low fidelity means low detail.** The purpose is to judge structure before
appearance, and appearance wins every time it is allowed in: a reviewer shown
finished colour and type stops evaluating the layout and starts reacting to the
look. So the drawing contains:

- **Grayscale only.** No brand colour, no accent, no status colours — unless colour
  *is* the feature (a colour picker, a status legend), and then only there.
- **No tokens and no project CSS.** Use the wireframe stylesheet below, pasted into
  the file's `<style>`. The mockup must not look like the product; if it does, it
  has become a design review.
- **No icons, imagery, shadows or decoration.** An image is a labelled box that says
  what goes there. An icon is its name in brackets: `[search]`.
- **Real words, not lorem ipsum.** Labels, headings and button text are part of the
  structure — "Create invoice" versus "Submit" is a decision worth reviewing.
- **Every size the spec names, as its own frame** — phone, then tablet, then desktop,
  stacked top to bottom, each drawn with its own markup. Size is structure, not
  detail: a phone composition that is only the desktop one narrower is the failure
  specs are written per size to prevent. A `same-as:` size is drawn anyway, so the
  claim can be seen to hold.
- **The primary state, plus any state that changes the structure.** An empty state
  that replaces a table, or an error that moves a form, is drawn. The rest stay
  listed in the spec. Do not draw what building will reveal: 2,000 contacts, a
  thirty-character name, a slow network — unless the feature is about exactly that.

```html
<style>
  /* Jig wireframe. Structure only: do not add colour, fonts, shadows or images. */
  *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body { margin: 0; padding: 24px; font: 16px/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif; color: #222; background: #f4f4f4; }
  .size { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 13px; color: #666; }
  .frame { background: #fff; border: 1px solid #999; margin: 0 0 40px; padding: 16px; overflow: hidden; }
  .frame[data-size="phone"] { width: 360px; }
  .frame[data-size="tablet"] { width: 768px; }
  .frame[data-size="desktop"] { width: 1280px; }
  .region { border: 1px dashed #aaa; padding: 12px; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .region > .name { display: block; font-size: 12px; color: #777; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .media { background: #ddd; color: #555; display: grid; place-items: center; min-height: 120px; }
  .field { display: block; width: 100%; min-height: 40px; border: 1px solid #888; background: #fff; }
  button { font: inherit; padding: 8px 16px; border: 1px solid #555; background: #fff; }
  button.primary { background: #444; color: #fff; border-color: #444; }
  h1 { font-size: 28px; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  h2 { font-size: 22px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  h3 { font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
</style>
```

Each frame is a `<section class="frame" data-size="phone">`, preceded by a
`<p class="size">` naming the size and its width. Regions carry the names the spec
uses, in a `.name` label, so the drawing and the spec can be read against each
other.

### 3. Put it in front of the user

Tell the user where the drawing is — the file path, or the Figma or Stitch link —
and how to open it. If the harness can render —
a browse skill, Playwright, a headless browser — render each frame and show them the
images; a drawing nobody looked at has not been reviewed.

Then ask **structural** questions, not whether they like it: is the most important
thing the first thing seen at each size? Does anything belong in a different group?
Is anything here that V1 does not need?

### 4. Every change goes into the spec first

When the review moves, adds, cuts or regroups anything, **edit the spec**, then
redraw from it. Never adjust the drawing alone. A mockup changed in review and not in
the spec has quietly become the real specification, and `make` would build from a
document that no longer describes what was approved.

A request for more — "add reactions while you're at it" — goes to `later:`, unless
the user explicitly moves it into V1. Then the spec's scope changes, and it is
confirmed again.

### 5. Record the outcome in the user's words

- The user approves → set `mockup: approved` in the spec, and `confirmed: true` if
  review changed it. Record where the approved drawing is in `mockup_at:` — the
  file path, or the Figma or Stitch link. Only their own response counts; your summary of the drawing,
  or no objection, does not.
- The user says to skip it → `mockup: skipped — <their reason>`. For a change small
  enough that drawing it costs more than building it, that is a reasonable call; it
  is still theirs to make.

Then stop. The next step is `{{command_prefix}}make`.

## make

**High-fidelity.** Build the actual page or feature from its confirmed spec — the
real tokens, components and copy, working.

### Refuse rather than guess

- No `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → say so and run `{{command_prefix}}spec`
  first. Do not write a quick spec of your own to satisfy the check; a spec you
  wrote and accepted in the same breath is the thing the confirmation exists to
  prevent.
- `confirmed: false` → the spec exists but nobody has agreed to it. Ask for
  confirmation and stop.
- `mockup: pending` → the feature has not been looked at. Run
  `{{command_prefix}}mockup`, or ask the user whether to skip it. Do not decide to
  skip it yourself.

### Build

**From the spec, never from the drawing.** Not the mockup's HTML, and not code
generated from a Figma or Stitch frame — both tools will offer that. The drawing
has fixed frame widths, grey boxes and no tokens. Turning it into the product is the
one way a disposable drawing becomes permanent code, carrying none of the rules or
tokens the spec was written against. If the spec and the
approved drawing disagree, the spec was not updated during review — stop and fix the
spec, rather than choosing between them.

Build V1 only. Nothing in `later:`, however little extra it looks.

Build the **phone** composition first, then add what `tablet` and `desktop`
change. Write base styles for the phone and add width as it is available — not
the desktop layout with overrides that take it apart again. A layout built wide
and subtracted from ends up correct at exactly the widths somebody checked.

Then the ordinary rules apply: tokens by semantic name, the relevant
`03-patterns.md` section for each component, `05-copy.md` for every string.

### Record what you changed

Building reveals that a spec was wrong somewhere — that is normal and expected.
**Silence is the failure, not the change.** Append to `deviations:`:

```yaml
deviations:
  - field: sizes.phone.form.steps
    from: 1
    to: 2
    why: "password reset needs an email round-trip; one screen cannot hold both"
```

A spec that quietly stops describing the page is worse than no spec: a later
review then validates the page against fiction and reports it clean.

### Finish

Run `check`, then the self-check at the end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md`,
and say which fields of the spec the built page satisfies and which it does not —
**at each size**. A page that satisfies `desktop` and was never looked at on a
phone has satisfied a third of its spec.

## critique

Scrutinise what was built against the rules and its spec. This is **not** a second `check`, and the difference is
not effort — it is evidence.

`check` reads files. `critique` looks at the page **and** reads the source
against the rules `check` has no detector for. A hierarchy failure is in no
file: it is in what the files produce together, so no amount of careful reading
finds it. That is what the render arm is for.

Every judgment rule says which pass owns it, in `pass:`. The corpus divides
three ways, and each part needs a different instrument:

| | rules | instrument |
|---|---|---|
| mechanical, has a `detector` | a small minority | `check`, deterministic |
| judgment, `pass: screen` | the render | arm A, looking at the page |
| judgment, `pass: code` | **the largest group** | arm C, reading the source |

The third one is the reason this command exists in its current form. `check`
judges only what a detector can decide, which is a fraction of the corpus. Every
other `pass: code` rule is judgment, and until something reads the source against
it, it is enforced by nothing but the building agent having remembered it. An
agent under load does not remember 60-odd rules; it consults the handful it
thought to search for, and ships the rest unchecked.

No two arms judge the same rule, which is why they can never hand a user
competing verdicts on one id.

### Refuse rather than guess

- The user named no surface → ask which screen.
- There is no `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → say so and continue **rules-only**,
  reporting `spec=missing`. Do not invent the spec the page should have had; a
  spec written after the page, from the page, agrees with it by construction.

### 1. Three assessments that cannot see each other

Run all three. **No arm may see another's output**, and none may see the
conversation that built the page. Running them in one head anchors them to each
other; do not shortcut it for cost, speed or context size.

- **A — the reader.** Delegate to a subagent. Give it the URL or file, the spec,
  and the corpus. It judges the `pass: screen` rules against the render. Do
  **not** give it the build conversation. If it needs that conversation, it is
  not reviewing the page, it is agreeing with itself.
- **B — the machine.** Run `check`. Deterministic, same input same output. It
  judges the rules that carry a `detector`, and nothing else — its own
  attestation says `judgment=not-run`, and that is a true statement about the
  rest of the corpus, not a formality.
- **C — the source reader.** A second subagent, separate from A. Give it the
  source files, the spec, and the corpus, and no render. It judges every
  `pass: code` judgment rule — the group `check` cannot reach.

**If you cannot delegate an arm, it is skipped. You do not run it yourself.** No
subagent available, a subagent that cannot read the project's files — either way
the arm reports `skipped` with the reason, in words, in the report. A live run had
an agent build a page and then run the reader arm on it in its own head; every
verdict it wrote cited what the spec *intended* rather than what the page *did*,
and its one finding asked the builder to confirm it. A skipped arm is an honest
gap. A self-run arm is a false review that looks like a real one.

Each reader is a subagent for the same reason a writer does not proofread their
own copy. An agent that built the page knows what it meant, and what it meant is
invisible in the artifact — which is the one thing a reviewer must not have.

A and C are kept apart for a narrower reason: an arm that has seen the render
stops reading the source and starts confirming the picture. They answer to
different evidence and must not share it.

### 1a. Arms A and C walk the index. They do not search it.

**This is not a style preference. It is the difference between covering the
corpus and sampling it.**

Load `rules.index.json`, filter to the pass the arm owns, and return a verdict
for **every id in that list**: `ok`, `finding`, or `n/a` with one line of
reason. A rule that does not apply is still answered — `n/a, this page has no
form` is a verdict.

Searching the corpus is how a *builder* works, and it is the right method there:
it finds the rules you can already name. It cannot find the rule for the mistake
you do not know you are making, which is the only kind of rule worth writing
down. A pass driven by search returns what the agent thought to look for, and
reports nothing about everything else — indistinguishable, in the output, from a
clean page.

The builder cannot do this. It is holding a brief, a spec and a half-written
page, and it will drop most of the corpus under that load. An arm with one job
can walk all of it, which is the whole reason the work is split.

**Never narrow the list by guessing relevance before reading.** Filter by `pass`,
and by mode where a rule is mode-gated. Nothing else. An arm that decides in
advance which rules are worth checking has reintroduced search with extra
steps.

### 1b. What makes a verdict real

Every one of these was produced by a live run of this command, by arms that had
been told to walk the index and believed they were doing it.

**An `n/a` must name what the rule is about.** Not "does not apply" — *what* does
not apply. `n/a, D-27 is about optical alignment and this page has no icons` is a
verdict. `n/a, rule context unavailable` is an absence wearing a verdict's shape,
and the difference is mechanically checkable: run
`{{command_prefix}}explain <id>` and the rule either resolves or it does not.

One run returned `C-68 — rule not found in accessible corpus` and
`D-27 — rule context unavailable`. Both resolve instantly. A third described
`D-25` as being about label spacing; it is about proximity hierarchy. None of
the three had been read. `C-68` is *non-interactive elements styled like
interactive ones* — adjacent to the only real finding that arm had raised.

**An id you cannot resolve is a hard stop.** If `explain` fails on an id that is
in the index, that is a corpus or install defect. Say so, loudly, and stop.
Recording it as a rule that happens not to apply buries a broken install inside a
clean-looking report.

**Never treat the count as a target.** The instruction is *return a verdict for
every id*; `<n>` is the consequence. An arm asked to reach a number will reach
it — that is how the three fabrications above were produced, in one turn, after a
reviewer asked for a short pass to be completed. **A pass that comes back short
is re-run, not topped up.**

**An arm that contradicts itself is re-run, not merged.** A live run's reader
arm said, in one report, that a toggle hard-codes `60px`/`34px` instead of tokens
(`A-07`) and that nothing on the page is hard-coded (`H-47`). Two opposite verdicts
on the same file are not a disagreement to report; they are an instrument that
cannot be trusted, and every other verdict it returned is suspect with them.

**Check the count before merging the arms.** Compare each arm's `<n>` against the
length of the filtered index. They should be equal. When they are not, name the
missing ids rather than reporting the total alone: one run reported
`rules_evaluated=67` against 69, and the two it had skipped were the two ids most
recently added to the corpus — the only two that could not be recalled from prior
knowledge of Jig. An arm asked to walk the index may walk its memory of the
index, and the ids it drops are the newest ones, which are the ones most in need
of enforcement. The id list is what exposes this; the total alone hides it.

### 2. Look at the page

If a browser is available this is **required**, not optional. Use whatever the
harness provides — a browse skill, Playwright, a headless browser. Reading the
source and imagining the page is not a render.

- **Render at every size the spec names** — phone at 360px, tablet at 768px,
  desktop at 1280px — and judge each size's fields against its own render. A
  `same-as:` claim is checked like any other field: render that size and see
  whether the composition really does hold.
- At every size, measure `document.documentElement.scrollWidth` against
  `clientWidth`. Greater means the page scrolls sideways at that width, which is a
  finding however good the rest of it looks.
- Compare the sizes. Did the phone change the **composition**, or only the
  **dimensions**? Reordering, stacking, collapsing, a different control — that is
  composition. The same layout with smaller numbers is not.
- Check the page is styled at all. A stylesheet that 404s renders a page that
  passes every file-based check ever written.
- Run the squint test from `L-01` against the render, not the analogue. The
  analogue exists for when there is no browser.

If no browser is available, say so in one line and use `L-01`'s analogue. Never
skip it silently, and never report a render you did not do.

### 3. Compare the page to its spec — not to the mockup

The mockup was disposable and anything the review decided went into the spec, so
the spec is the only thing a built page answers to.

For each size, for each frontmatter field, say whether the page rendered at that
size satisfies it. A difference
is a finding **unless** `deviations:` already explains it — that is what the
recorded deviation is for. A difference explained by neither is the more serious
finding, because it means the spec has quietly stopped describing the page.

### 4. Report

Merge both assessments into one report, keyed by rule id, ordered by severity.
Say explicitly where the two agree, what only the machine found, what only the
reader found, and which machine findings are false positives. **The disagreement
is information, not noise to smooth over.**

Say what is right as well as what is wrong — two or three things, and why they
work. A review that only lists faults tells the reader nothing about what to
preserve while fixing them.

Then the attestation:

```text
JIG_CRITIQUE: version=<version> mode=<mode> surface=<name> spec=<confirmed|missing|unconfirmed> rendered=<yes|no> screen=<ran|skipped>:<n> code=<ran|skipped>:<n> mechanical=<pass|fail|skipped>:<n> warnings=<n>
```

Three counts because there are three arms, and each can fail independently.
`mechanical=` is `check`'s result and uses `check`'s own field name deliberately,
so the two records line up. `screen=` and `code=` are the two reader arms, and
their `<n>` is **the number of rules the arm returned a verdict for** — not the
number of findings. An arm that walked the index reports the size of the list it
walked; an arm reporting far fewer did not walk it, and the number is where that
shows.

`code=skipped:0` is the state this command was rebuilt to make visible. It used
to be unsayable: the largest group of rules in the corpus went unjudged and the
report looked complete.

`rendered=no` is not a detail. Without it a clean report reads as "this page is
good" when it can only mean "the files are good" — and a page whose stylesheet
never loaded satisfies every file-based check there is.

`rendered=yes` means **every size the spec names** was rendered. If only some
were, the report names which, and says what was not seen.

**`rendered=yes` requires an artefact.** A screenshot, a rendered dump, something
that exists because a browser ran. Reading the source and reasoning about how it
would look is the `L-01` analogue, and the analogue is `rendered=no`. A live run
of this command emitted `rendered=yes` on a machine with no browser installed and
no image written anywhere — turning an honest limitation into a false assurance,
on the one field a reader leans on hardest. If you did not see the page, say you
did not see the page.

## decide

Write `DECISIONS.md` beside the token layer — `jig/DECISIONS.md` by default, or
wherever `brand` in `{{config_file}}` puts the token files.

Designing and building are blocked until this exists: `spec` refuses to start
without it, and `mockup` and `make` need a confirmed spec. Nothing else is blocked —
`install`, `init`, `check`, `explain` and `critique` all run without it. `init`
comes first, because this file sits beside the token files `init` writes.

### What belongs in it, and what does not

The token layer already holds **values**, and `{{config_file}}` already holds
**mode**. Neither can hold a **reason**. This file is the reasons.

- ✅ *"Our accent arrives as a whole field or not at all — a full-bleed section,
  a solid button, a filled state. Never a 2px underline, never an icon tint.
  Its authority comes from arriving in quantity, rarely."*
- ❌ *"The accent is `#FF6347`."* — that is a token. Restating it here creates
  two places to change it, and they will disagree.
- ❌ *"Use 4.5:1 contrast."* — that is `C-19`, and it is true of every project.
  A universal rule copied into a project file is a rule that can rot locally.

The test: **could this be derived from the tokens, the mode, or a numbered rule?**
If yes, it is already written down somewhere better. If no, it is a decision, and
this is where it lives.

**`decide` is product-wide. `spec` is per screen.** Ask what holds on every
screen. What one screen contains, who arrives at it, what its FAQ asks, where
something sits on it — those are `spec`'s questions. Asking them here makes the
user answer them twice, and a live run did exactly that: one agent gathered a
page's FAQ and arrival paths under `decide`, the other under `spec`.

One kind of placement **is** a decision: something that must sit in the same
place on every screen, such as where the menu button lives. That is taste, not
something a rule can settle, and it belongs here once. Placement on a single
screen is not — and do not ask the owner where an element should go on a screen
at all. If the design system has a position, it is in the rules; if it has none,
`spec` settles it.

### How to write it

**Interview, do not infer.** Two or three questions per round, then wait. What
you are looking for is the places where this project has already chosen
something, or will have to:

- Round 1 — what is this product, and who is it for in a sentence the team would
  recognise? What should it never look like? Name real products, not adjectives.
- Round 2 — for each thing the tokens already set, is there a rule about *how* it
  is used? Where does the brand colour go, and where is it forbidden? What earns
  emphasis?
- Round 3 — what has the team already argued about, or reversed? A decision with
  a history is the one most worth recording, because it is the one most likely to
  be re-made wrongly.

**All three rounds run.** Do not write the file after round 2 because the answers
look complete. Round 3 asks for the one kind of decision a user does not volunteer
unprompted — the ones with a history — and a live run skipped it for exactly that
reason. In the arm that did ask, round 3 is where the unresolved items surfaced.

**Write each decision as a named rule with its reason attached**, in the project's
own vocabulary:

```markdown
### The Stamp Rule

The accent appears as a whole field or not at all. A full-bleed section, a solid
button, a filled active state — never a 2px underline, never a small icon tint,
never a gradient stop. Its authority comes from arriving in quantity, rarely.

**Why:** thinly spread, it reads as decoration and stops meaning anything. The
audit test: if a coloured element is smaller than a section band, it is probably
wrong.
```

A name gives the team something to cite in review. A reason lets a future agent
tell when the rule does not apply, which a bare instruction never can.

### Finish

Show it and ask the user to confirm it is right before you stop. Leave no
`[TODO]` markers — the gate treats them as an unwritten file, correctly, because
a placeholder decision is not a decision.
